@namespace SpaceSails.Client.Pages
@implements IHandleEvent
@using ArchiveCard = SpaceSails.Client.Pages.Map.ArchiveCard
@using System.Globalization
@using SpaceSails.Client.Rendering
@using SpaceSails.Contracts
@using SpaceSails.Core
@using SpaceSails.Core.Interior
@*
    #251 item 1 · THE ARCHIVE VISION CARD, ON ITS OWN FILE.

    Moved out of Map.razor by PURE MOTION — the block between the two fences below is the block that was in
    the page, character for character. Every page member it reads is a [Parameter] with the SAME NAME (the
    fields keep their underscore, the methods come across as delegates), which is what lets the markup move
    without a rewrite and lets the suite's source guards read it through MapMarkup exactly as before.

    The `@if` that opens it stays in Map.razor: the page keeps its paint order in one place, and a closed
    surface still renders nothing. IHandleEvent repeats the page's own override (Map.Sim.cs) — without it
    Blazor would put an automatic re-render back on every event in here, which the page turns off because
    the HUD's refresh belongs to OnTick's 200 ms throttle. The .razor.css beside this file carries no rules;
    it exists to give the component Map.razor.css's scope (#251 item 3 moves the rules).

    The `@{ }` around the fences is not decoration: the block used to sit inside `@if (…) { … }`,
    which is a Razor CODE block, and a code block is where its first statements are legal. The braces
    put the moved lines back in the context they were written in; they are outside the fences, so what
    the source guards read is still exactly the lines that were in the page.
*@
@* ── THE PAGE'S OWN NOTE ON THIS SURFACE, moved here with it (#251 item 1) and not touched by a
   word: it is the paragraph that stood above this block in Map.razor. It sits ABOVE the fence, not
   inside it, so that a source guard slicing the block by text goes on reading markup and not prose.
   ── *@
    @* THE ARCHIVE NODE — what the thing in the hold gave back. See docs/features/the-archive-node.md.

       House rules this card keeps, and they are the whole feature:
         · The image is the EVIDENCE, never the conclusion. There is no caption telling you what the
           handlers are; the game does not know either.
         · The arithmetic is VISIBLE. A bad result is only bearable when you can read exactly why it went
           that way, so the modifier stack is printed rather than hidden.
         · The COLLAR line only appears on the bands that got close enough to read it. Knowing whose
           pattern is in the spar is bought with a bad throw, never given with a good one.
         · Nothing here explains anything. It never confirms what Nebula keeps, and it never says a word
           about the Old Ones. *@
    @* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       #997 wave 3 · THE .view-object FAMILY IS ON THE SHELL — all of it, and this is the first of the ten.

       Twelve cards in this client are rooted on `.view-object`. #997 put the rep's pitch on the shell and
       #997 wave 2 put the walk-in beside him; the other TEN went on hand-rolling the same three lines each
       — a `@@onclick:stopPropagation` to hold the scrim off, a `<button class="view-object-close">`, and a
       `</div>`. Ten copies of one gesture is how the minimise came to be written three times.

       So every one of them takes the same three parameters, and this comment is the only one written:
       `Frame="OverlayFrame.Bare"` (the family's foot is pinned by `::deep .view-object > .view-object-close`
       — the way out has to be a DIRECT child of the card, which is what Bare guarantees and what Hosted's
       `display: contents` would silently break), `StopClicks="true"` (the stopPropagation that was typed on
       the div), and `Dismiss="OverlayDismiss.Close"` wired to the page's OWN close verb — the same method
       the backdrop behind it already calls.

       NOT ONE WORD MOVED. Each card's face and title are the exact string its own button carried, because
       this family's idiom is a WORDED way out and not a ✕ — "Get your eyes off it", "…go on", "Stow it",
       "Pocket it", "Leave it". And not one of the ten needed wave 2's stranding rule: the walk-in's ✕ had
       to run her own "no" because closing her card would have left a woman at the table with nothing left
       to answer. Nothing here is standing anywhere. Every one of these cards is a THING the captain picked
       up, walked up to or switched on, and putting it down is already what its button did. *@

@{
@* ── MARKUP BEGINS · verbatim from Map.razor ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── *@
        <div class="view-object-backdrop" @onclick="() => Dismiss(CloseArchiveCard)">
            <OverlayShell class="view-object archive-vision" StopClicks="true"
                          Frame="OverlayFrame.Bare" Dismiss="OverlayDismiss.Close"
                          DismissClass="view-object-close" DismissFace="Get your eyes off it"
                          DismissTitle="Get your eyes off it"
                          OnClose="() => Dismiss(CloseArchiveCard)">
                <div class="view-object-title">@vision.Title</div>
                <img class="view-object-img" src="@vision.Art" alt="@vision.Title" onerror="this.style.display='none'" />
                <div class="archive-band">@vision.BandLine</div>
                <div class="view-object-caption">@vision.Lore</div>
                @if (vision.Collar is { Length: > 0 } collar)
                {
                    <div class="archive-collar">@collar</div>
                }
                <div class="archive-dice">🎲 @vision.Dice</div>
            </OverlayShell>
        </div>
@* ── MARKUP ENDS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── *@
}

@code {
    // ── WHAT THESE PARAMETERS ARE ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    //
    // #251 item 1 · Every [Parameter] below carries a member of the page under THE MEMBER'S OWN NAME —
    // a field keeps its underscore, a method arrives as a delegate with its own signature, a variable the
    // page's `@if` binds arrives under the name the `@if` gave it. That is the whole trick of this
    // refactor: it is what let the markup above move out of Map.razor without a single character of it
    // changing, and what lets the suite's source guards read it through MapMarkup exactly as they read it
    // when it lived in the page. A parameter that needs saying more than that says it on its own line.

    [Parameter] public Action CloseArchiveCard { get; set; } = default!;
    [Parameter] public Func<Action, Task> Dismiss { get; set; } = default!;
    [Parameter] public ArchiveCard vision { get; set; } = default!;

    // The page's own event dispatch, repeated: no automatic re-render per event.
    Task IHandleEvent.HandleEventAsync(EventCallbackWorkItem callback, object? arg) => callback.InvokeAsync(arg);
}
